<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">22 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 22 October, from 794 to 1962.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">794 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,232 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Kanmu moves Japan's capital to Heian-kyō</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the 22nd day of the tenth month, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> formally moved his court to the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. It remained the imperial seat for over a thousand years; the Jidai Matsuri festival marks the date.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">906 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,120 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ahmad ibn Kayghalagh leads an Abbasid raid into Byzantine lands</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Abbasid officer Ahmad ibn Kayghalagh led the annual raid against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> with the local governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> ibn Baradu. According to al-Tabari, they defeated the Byzantines and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys River</a>, taking 4,000 or 5,000 captives.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1303">1303 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">723 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nicola Boccasini is elected pope as Benedict XI</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Nicola Boccasini was elected pope as Benedict XI, succeeding Boniface VIII, who had died weeks earlier, shortly after his seizure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a> in his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>. Benedict's pontificate lasted until his death in July 1304.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1383 CE <span class="tih-ago">643 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ferdinand I of Portugal dies, opening a succession crisis</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King Ferdinand I of Portugal died without a legitimate male heir, ending the male line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a>. His daughter Beatrice, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>, claimed the throne, but many Portuguese backed John, Master of Aviz.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1383-1385-portuguese-interregnum">1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1721">1721 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">305 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Peter the Great takes the title of emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, the Russian Senate conferred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a> the titles 'the Great' and Emperor of All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> (22 October, Old Style). Russia was now formally an empire and a European great power.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1797">1797 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">229 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">André-Jacques Garnerin makes the first parachute descent from a balloon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Over the Parc Monceau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, André-Jacques Garnerin cut his parachute free of a hydrogen balloon at about 1,000 metres. He landed safely despite violent swinging, the first frameless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> parachute jump.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1836">1836 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">190 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sam Houston becomes the first president of the Republic of Texas</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-houston">Sam Houston</a>, victor of San Jacinto, was inaugurated as the first elected president of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> at Columbia. Texas remained an independent republic until joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1845.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1962">1962 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">64 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Kennedy tells Americans of Soviet missiles in Cuba</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In a televised address, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> revealed that Soviet nuclear missile sites were being built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and announced a naval 'quarantine' of the island. The crisis brought the superpowers closer to nuclear war than ever before.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 22 October <span class="n">7</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-ix-duke-of-aquitaine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-ix-duke-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William IX, Duke of Aquitaine</span><span class="tp">1071–1126</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/javier-de-burgos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/javier-de-burgos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Javier de Burgos</span><span class="tp">1778–1848</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/franz-liszt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Franz Liszt</span><span class="tp">1811–1886</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-riel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis Riel</span><span class="tp">1844–1885</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/koos-de-la-rey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Koos de la Rey</span><span class="tp">1847–1914</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bao-dai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Bảo Đại</span><span class="tp">1913–1997</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/javier-milei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/javier-milei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Javier Milei</span><span class="tp">b. 1970</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 22 October <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/itzamnaaj-kawiil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itzamnaaj-kawiil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Itzamnaaj K'awiil</span><span class="tp">673–726</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-prince-of-orange"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-iv-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William IV, Prince of Orange</span><span class="tp">1711–1751</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/geraldine-of-albania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/geraldine-of-albania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Geraldine of Albania</span><span class="tp">1915–2002</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gustavo-gutierrez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Gustavo Gutiérrez</span><span class="tp">1928–2024</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
